A||||10||SteveJ||Decklid intechangable 84 and Later yrs?||||5||
Z||000000||SteveJ||02-23-2001||03:20 PM||||In another thread (Opinions Wanted. . .) Tweek shows his car which is an '84:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/b-w_rear.jpg"> <BR>Notice the rear deck which was modified by a previous owner into what looks like a later year rear deck.<BR>This I would like to do. Does anyone know for certain that this is a bolt on exchange and not something the previous owner had to fight with to get right?<BR>Tweek and I think think its a bolt on. Anybody know for certain?||||reg||5
Z||000001||DXR_DAD||02-23-2001||03:40 PM||||Yes, bolt it on!!!  It will fit. You will need the grills. I should say it's an exchange bolt on, just get a later year deck lid with grills. no mods needed<p>[This message has been edited by DXR_DAD (edited 02-23-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000002||SteveJ||02-23-2001||03:51 PM||||Excellent, No more wet engine.||||reg||1
Z||000003||webbee||02-23-2001||04:20 PM||||That may be an '84 lid with a Mustang scoop bolted/glassed on. The lovers interchange with the stock solid units.<P>Go for it!  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||DXR_DAD||02-23-2001||06:03 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=99075&a=723601&p=18231066&Sequence=1&res=high"> <P>"MUSTANG STYLE"||||reg||12
Z||000005||Tigger||02-23-2001||06:38 PM||||Yep, I did this to my '84.  Just make sure it's a decklid from another notchback GT.  The lock cylinder can be transfered from one to the other easy so you can use the same key.<P>If it has the electric decklid release, make sure it has the same connector plug, if it does, it should be a plug-n-play swap.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tweek||02-23-2001||10:53 PM||||Nope, mine definately isnt Stang style, just a hump. Quick question here, the cars that came with this.. 85 and up notchbacks.. do you guys just let the battery get rained on?? The previous owner of mine just left that flat plate over on the right... and put a grill on the left. I kinda like this setup, but it would look better with two grills....<P>-Ted<p>[This message has been edited by Tweek (edited 02-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||02-24-2001||01:51 AM||||Just let the battery get rained on.  I took out the extra stamped sheet metal grill that's riveted on underneath the GT grill.  Snow has a tendancy to pile up underneath there now, but I've never had a problem.  Lets a little more air flow through there, too.||||reg||1
Z||000008||FieroJoe||02-24-2001||07:17 PM||||I was going to remove the stamped sheet too, but had a question about getting the battery wet.  I guess if I do that then I should probably remove and paint my battery tray to keep it from rusting (not a touch of rust yet!), and just keep the terminals clean and corrosion free?<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR>AIM: Fiero87311, Snowboarder0311<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Will||02-24-2001||09:01 PM||||If you're really worried about your battery getting wet, then move it to the front compartment.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Standard||02-26-2001||02:01 AM||||Yeah, I'd definatly rust-proof the battery tray.  Mine is almost completly gone, but it was like that when I got the car.  Seems to be a matter of luck with how much or how little your tray rusts out...  haven't noticed too much of a problem with the battery terminals, but it's always a good idea to keep them clean.||||reg||1
